		<link href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=37032#p37032"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Lightweight engine for simulating dice?]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=37032#p37032"><![CDATA[
I'm making a simple dice game and would like to have animated dice rolls but don't really want a full blown physics engine so was wondering what the best approach would be given the simplified constraints:<br><br> * Only unit cube rigid bodies (possibly other regular polyhedrons later on)<br> * Collision planes for the floor and walls<br> * Only a few rigid bodies to simulate (~5 dice)<br><br>I am looking for the most simple, robust method that looks acceptable.<br><br>I've tried a few approaches - a point/stick verlet model, a basic single contact impulse model, but it's proving quite difficult to keep things simple and stable.<br><br>Any ideas?<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=11629">GameSmith</a> — Sat Feb 13, 2016 12:03 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
